Subject: [Backport] CVE-2026-113097: Use after free in FileSystem (2/2)

Manual partial backport of patch originally reviewd on
https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/c/chromium/src/+/8302993:
Block handle use in third-party contexts

The File System Access API restricts file picker dialogs and permission
requests to first-party contexts. However, the permission model
decouples permission grant tracking from handle execution contexts.
`ChromeFileSystemAccessPermissionContext` caches permission grants keyed
strictly by origin, allowing operational methods in
`FileSystemAccessHandleBase` such as `AsBlob()` and
`RunWithPermission()` to proceed immediately when an origin already
holds `PermissionStatus::GRANTED`.

Consequently, cross-origin iframes obtaining handles via `postMessage()`
or `document.requestStorageAccess({indexedDB: true})` can read, write,
and observe granted host directories without triggering third-party
context blocks or displaying permission prompts.
This occurs because:
* `PermissionGrantImpl::RequestPermission()` evaluates active grants and
  persistent permissions before validating the requesting frame
  hierarchy against the grant origin.
* `FileSystemAccessHandleBase::GetReadPermissionStatus()`,
  `GetWritePermissionStatus()`, and `DoRequestPermission()` query shared
  grant status without evaluating whether the bound handle executes in a
  third-party context.
* Handle read methods including `AsBlob()`, `GetFile()`,
  `GetDirectoryResolved()`, and `GetEntries()`, along with
  `FileSystemAccessObserverHost`, check permission status without
  verifying execution context.

This CL enforces third-party execution context restrictions across both
the embedder permission boundary and the content handle boundary, while
preserving user Drag-and-Drop capabilities:

Third-Party Context Detection & Embedder Hardening:
* Adds `FileSystemAccessHandleBase::IsThirdPartyContext()` to detect
  non-sandboxed file system paths bound to third-party storage keys,
  exempting partitioned Origin Private File System paths under
  `storage::kFileSystemTypeTemporary`.
* Validates `RenderFrameHost` and rejects cross-origin or third-party
  iframes with `PermissionRequestOutcome::kThirdPartyContext` at the
  start of `PermissionGrantImpl::RequestPermission()`, prior to
  inspecting active or persistent permission statuses.
* Rejects `DoRequestPermission()` with `kSecurityError` in third-party
  contexts, preventing prompts from being shown.

Ensure Drag-and-Drop still supported in Third-Party Contexts:
* Mints a `Fixed(GRANTED)` read grant and `Fixed(DENIED)` write grant in
  `FileSystemAccessManagerImpl::GetSharedHandleStateForNonSandboxedPath()`
  for drag-and-drop gestures in third-party contexts, scoping transient
  read authorization exclusively to explicit user drops while denying
  reloaded storage or ambient origin access.
* Updates `GetReadPermissionStatus()` to report
  `PermissionStatus::GRANTED` in third-party contexts if the handle
  holds an active `GRANTED` read grant (minted for user drops), and
  `DENIED` otherwise.
* Updates `GetWritePermissionStatus()` and
  `GetReadWritePermissionStatus()` to unconditionally report `DENIED` in
  third-party contexts.

Operational Handle & Observation Guards:
* Rejects operational read methods (`AsBlob()`, `GetFile()`,
  `GetDirectoryResolved()`, and `GetEntries()`) with `kSecurityError` in
  third-party contexts unless the handle holds an active read grant.
* Downgrades non-sandboxed grants to `Fixed(DENIED)` when a transfer
  token is redeemed in a third-party context
  (`FileSystemAccessTransferTokenImpl::CreateFileHandle()` and
  `CreateDirectoryHandle()`). This covers handles obtained via
  `postMessage()` and `document.requestStorageAccess({indexedDB: true})`.
  As a result, a handle transferred into a third-party context, including
  a dropped handle posted to a worker, is not readable, and a handle sent
  back from a third-party context to a first-party context stays denied.
* Rejects
  `FileSystemAccessObserverHost::DidResolveTransferTokenToObserve()`
  with `kSecurityError` in third-party contexts.

